---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/what-is-base64-and-when-to-use-it.html">
<aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
    <div class="wrap article">
      <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Base64</p>
      <h1>What Is Base64? A Plain Guide to Text Codes</h1>
      <p class="lead">Base64 turns a file or piece of text into ordinary letters and numbers. It lets data travel safely through systems that only handle plain text. This guide explains what it is and where you will see it.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>What Base64 does</h2>
<p>Some systems can only carry plain letters and numbers. Base64 changes any file or text into a set of such characters, so it can travel through those systems safely. It does not make the data smaller or secret.</p>
<h2>Why the result is larger</h2>
<p>Every three bytes of the original become four characters. The result is about a third larger than the original.</p>
<h2>Where you will see it</h2>
<ul>
<li>Small pictures built into a web page.</li>
<li>Email attachments.</li>
<li>Login details passed between websites.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Base64 is not a lock</h2>
<p>Anyone can change Base64 back to the original straight away. Never use it to hide a password or private information.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The text Hi is two bytes, 48 and 69 in hex. Written in binary and regrouped into six-bit chunks, they become the four Base64 characters SGk=, where the equals sign pads the end.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Is Base64 a form of encryption?</summary><p>No. Anyone can decode Base64 instantly, so it must not be used to hide passwords or private data.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Why does Base64 make files bigger?</summary><p>Every three bytes become four characters, so the text is about a third larger than the original.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Where is Base64 used?</summary><p>In data URLs for small images, in email attachments, in some API tokens and in HTTP basic authentication headers.</p></details>
      </section>
